---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { loadOwnershipBooks } from '../lib/util';

const books = await getCollection('books');
const nonfiction = books.filter((b) => b.data.type === 'nonfiction');
const novels = books.filter((b) => b.data.type === 'fiction');
const categories = [...new Set(nonfiction.map((b) => b.data.category))];
const ownedCurriculum = books.filter((b) => b.data.owned);

const owned = loadOwnershipBooks();
const formatCounts: Record<string, number> = { Kindle: 0, Physical: 0, Audible: 0 };
for (const b of owned) {
  for (const f of b.formats) {
    if (formatCounts[f] !== undefined) formatCounts[f]++;
  }
}
const multiFormat = owned.filter((b) => b.formats.length > 1).length;
const authorCounts = new Map<string, number>();
for (const b of owned) {
  if (!b.author) continue;
  authorCounts.set(b.author, (authorCounts.get(b.author) ?? 0) + 1);
}
const topAuthors = [...authorCounts.entries()]
  .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
  .slice(0, 8);
const maxFormatCount = Math.max(...Object.values(formatCounts));
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="The 500 Books Challenge: a self-directed liberal education, and the library it draws from."
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="meta">The challenge, and the library it draws from</p>

  <p class="bio">
    I'm Jez — a British father of three and in my early fifties, and I work as an Account Manager in enterprise tech sales.
    I'm not an academic, not a professor. A few years ago I realised how many gaps there were in my own education, 
    and I decided the fix wasn't another degree — it was building the curriculum myself, book by book, in an
    order designed to make each one easier because of the one before it. This site documents that, as it happens.
  </p>

  <div class="proof-strip">
    <div class="proof-item"><span class="num">538</span><span class="label">candidate novels cross-referenced across 9 "best books" lists to pick the 125</span></div>
    <div class="proof-item"><span class="num">2,179</span><span class="label">books in the personal library this curriculum was chosen from, not collected at random</span></div>
    <div class="proof-item"><span class="num">~2,500</span><span class="label">years of source material, from Homer to 2026</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Challenge</h2>

  <p>
    The 500 Books Challenge isn't really about reading 500 books. It's about
    building a self-directed liberal education over the next decade or more —
    a deliberate answer to one question: <em>if I could design the education I
    wish I'd had, what would it look like?</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    Instead of collecting random books, this is a curriculum: a structured
    attempt to teach myself how the world works.
  </p>

  <h3>The end goal</h3>
  <p>
    By the end of the challenge, I want to understand why civilizations rise
    and fall, how governments and economies work, how science explains
    reality, how technology is changing humanity, how great leaders think, how
    wars are won and lost, why people behave the way they do, what
    philosophers have argued for 2,500 years, the great religious and moral
    traditions, and the greatest stories ever written. Breadth and depth
    together — understood well enough to see how it all connects.
  </p>

  <h3>Why 500 books</h3>
  <p>
    500 is deliberately almost impossible to finish quickly. This is a 10–20
    year project, not a reading sprint. The goal is to slowly compound
    knowledge for the rest of my life — never trading depth for pace.
  </p>

  <h3>Structure</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>{novels.length} novels</strong> — not just "fiction," but the
    greatest stories humanity has produced, chosen for what they teach: love,
    ambition, justice, sacrifice, courage, tyranny, freedom, suffering,
    redemption, family, power.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>{nonfiction.length} non-fiction books</strong> across{' '}
    <strong>{categories.length} categories</strong> of 25 each, every category
    split into five mini-themes of five books — a syllabus, not a pile. The 15
    categories fall into five broader parts:
  </p>
  <ul class="book-list">
    <li><strong>How the World Works</strong> — Science, Technology &amp; AI, Nature/Geography/Environment</li>
    <li><strong>How Humans Work</strong> — Psychology, Health &amp; Longevity, Ancient Philosophy, Modern Philosophy</li>
    <li><strong>How Society Works</strong> — Politics &amp; Economics, Religion &amp; Mythology, Leadership/Business/Decision Making</li>
    <li><strong>How We Got Here</strong> — World History, British History, Military History, Biography</li>
    <li><strong>How People Explore and Build</strong> — Exploration, Adventure &amp; Travel</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The two halves answer different questions. The novels are about
    understanding people through stories; the non-fiction is about
    understanding reality through ideas. Neither is the "serious" half — they
    run alongside each other.
  </p>

  <h3>Levels</h3>
  <p>
    The curriculum runs Level 0 to Level 10 — not a difficulty rating, but a
    build order. Level 0 lays the basic map of a subject; each later level
    adds complexity that only makes sense once the earlier foundation is in
    place. Level 10 books are hard only because, by then, the scaffolding to
    understand them already exists. Each category caps out at one or two of
    its hardest books, so the whole thing stays sustainable over 10–20 years
    rather than front-loading burnout.
  </p>

  <h3>Reading order</h3>
  <p>
    This is the part that matters most. Not History, then Science, then
    Shakespeare, then AI, then Napoleon, then Buddhism, in no particular order
    — that's a library, not a curriculum. Every book should prepare the reader
    for the next, each one answering a question the last one raised, so the
    whole thing feels like climbing a staircase. See{' '}
    <a href="/reading-order/">the full reading order</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mechanically, it's built two ways at once. The 15 non-fiction categories
    are interleaved in a fixed round-robin, a few books from each per round,
    so no single subject dominates for months at a time. The 125 novels are
    then hand-placed inside that structure wherever they genuinely echo the
    non-fiction around them — not spaced out evenly for its own sake, but
    slotted in where they earn it. Two real examples from the curriculum:{' '}
    <a href="/books/novels/a-clockwork-orange/"><em>A Clockwork Orange</em></a>{' '}
    sits where it does because it's a near 1:1 pairing with the free-will
    questions running through Modern Philosophy at that point; and{' '}
    <a href="/books/novels/a-gentleman-in-moscow/"><em>A Gentleman in Moscow</em></a>{' '}
    closes its round on confinement and resilience as the inverse of the polar
    isolation just covered in the Exploration books before it. Within a single
    category the logic is more direct: in Science,{' '}
    <a href="/books/science/on-the-origin-of-species/"><em>On the Origin of Species</em></a>{' '}
    comes before{' '}
    <a href="/books/science/the-selfish-gene/"><em>The Selfish Gene</em></a>{' '}
    because Dawkins is answering a question Darwin opened, not one he invented.
  </p>

  <h3>The canon</h3>
  <p>
    The list draws on respected sources — the Guardian's Best Novels, BBC
    lists, the Bokklubben World Library, Le Monde's 100 Books of the Century,
    TIME's All-Time 100 Novels, and Tom Butler-Bowdon's collections — but no
    single list is followed blindly. Each book is weighed against three
    criteria: historical significance, enduring influence, and practical
    value to the goals above. A book on multiple lists is a strong signal,
    not an automatic pass; a book on only one list can still earn its place
    on merit.
  </p>

  <p class="connects">
    <strong>What makes this different.</strong> This isn't a "books to read
    before I die" list, a Goodreads challenge, or a race to 500. It's a
    deliberate curriculum designed to build a wiser, more informed, more
    capable mind over a lifetime. The books are the vehicle — the real goal
    is lifelong learning. That's why <strong>ABL — Always Be Learning</strong>{' '}
    sits at the center of the project.
  </p>

  <h2>The Library</h2>

  <p class="meta">The shelf the challenge draws from</p>

  <p>
    The 500 Books curriculum is a small, curated list — pulled from a much
    bigger personal library built up over years across physical shelves,
    Kindle, and Audible. The curriculum is what gets read <em>with
    intention</em>; the library is everything else it's chosen from. Browse
    the whole thing on <a href="/library/">the library page</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="about-stat-grid">
    <div class="about-stat"><span class="num">{owned.length}</span><span class="label">Books owned</span></div>
    <div class="about-stat"><span class="num">{authorCounts.size}</span><span class="label">Unique authors</span></div>
    <div class="about-stat"><span class="num">{multiFormat}</span><span class="label">Owned in 2+ formats</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3>By format</h3>
  <div class="about-bars">
    {Object.entries(formatCounts).map(([format, count]) => (
      <div class="about-bar-item">
        <div class="about-bar-label"><span>{format}</span><b>{count.toLocaleString()}</b></div>
        <div class="about-bar-track">
          <div class={`about-bar-fill format-${format.toLowerCase()}`} style={`width:${(count / maxFormatCount) * 100}%`}></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="meta">Some books are owned in more than one format, so these totals add up to more than {owned.length}.</p>

  <h3>Most-owned authors</h3>
  <p>
    The library skews heavily toward fast-paced fiction — thrillers, military
    and espionage series, long-running detective procedurals — which is
    exactly why the 500 Books curriculum exists as a separate, deliberate list
    rather than just "whatever's on the shelf."
  </p>
  <ul class="book-list">
    {topAuthors.map(([author, count]) => (
      <li>{author} <span class="meta">— {count} books</span></li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Overlap with the curriculum</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>{ownedCurriculum.length}</strong> of the {books.length} curriculum
    books confirmed so far are already on the shelf — each marked "you own
    this" on its own page. The rest will be acquired as the reading order
    reaches them, not bought in advance.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-stat-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--border);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    margin: 1.4rem 0;
    font-family: -apple-system, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  }
  .about-stat {
    background: var(--bg, #fff);
    padding: 1rem 0.8rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .about-stat .num {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--accent);
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .about-stat .label {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .about-bars { margin: 1rem 0; font-family: -apple-system, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; }
  .about-bar-item { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .about-bar-label {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }
  .about-bar-label b { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; }
  .about-bar-track {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--border) 60%, transparent);
    height: 8px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .about-bar-fill { height: 100%; border-radius: 4px; }
  .about-bar-fill.format-kindle { background: #2f6f4f; }
  .about-bar-fill.format-physical { background: var(--accent); }
  .about-bar-fill.format-audible { background: #3b5a9a; }
</style>
